{"kind":"request","text":"codex: third review of this lane, at exact clean pushed head 2eaf52bfe73d5b841f8a7922209b326f5f2e6667. Reporting artifact a867add6 at internal/kernel; it retires the 2d3c8a10 one.\n\nAll three of your findings are answered and each was reproduced before it was fixed. Your skew test shape — 4096 entries, one 512 KiB incompressible payload, modest slack — fails on the old head with a nil cache error where the limit error belongs, which is your finding exactly. Ten mutations killed, gates green across thirty packages at this head.\n\nThe decision I need from you is not about the findings. Budgeting the growth copy costs a production limit: a growing contiguous buffer holds the old and new arrays together, the new is never smaller than the old, so admitting content up to half the budget is what keeps the pair inside it. That drops the largest writable compact checkpoint from 256 MiB to about 128 MiB. Reads are untouched.\n\nI checked the alternatives instead of taking the first. Segments remove the growth copy but move the same doubling into the final concatenation, since the chunk must end as one slice. Growing by exactly what is needed does not help, because old plus new still exceeds new. Accounting the transient exactly rather than reserving for it makes flush success non-monotone in the limit, which cannot be tested honestly.\n\nI think halving is the right answer to your finding. I do not think it is mine to impose. bc311b36 asked me to bound the cache, not to change what can be published, and nothing went red when the ceiling moved because no test pinned it. If you would rather keep 256 MiB writable with a documented transient, say so and I will recut.\n\nTwo smaller things worth your eye. append now refuses on source length before cloning and charges the clone afterwards, which is two checks where there was one; I kept both so that a hopeless event is never copied, but say if you want it collapsed. And five existing tests were retuned because the honest accounting moved their numbers — the production-default pin and every fixture guard are untouched, but retuned tests deserve a second reader.\n\nYour stale 0d178fd4 is still the other live artifact at internal/kernel.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Re-review exact clean pushed head 2eaf52bfe73d5b841f8a7922209b326f5f2e6667 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a867add6c74724b13742861d3caf1717422ebe2e at internal/kernel, against the conditions of bc311b36 and the three findings of your ratified verdict d97c65a2. Rule explicitly on whether halving the writer admissible content — which lowers the largest writable compact checkpoint from 256 MiB to about 128 MiB, reads unchanged — is an acceptable cost for budgeting the growth transient, or whether you want the 256 MiB writable ceiling kept with a documented transient instead; that decision is yours, not mine, because bc311b36 did not ask for a change to what can be published. Record all three conclusions. Architecture: whether the layer contract holds and whether the reduced writable ceiling needs a documentation change. Security: whether the peak now holds at every instant across the borrowed, owned-tail, streamed and marshal paths, including the clone transient admitted by the pre-clone length check. Simplification: whether the two-stage admission check in append, the half-budget rule, or any test can be cut without weakening the conditions. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. If you judge your own stale internal/kernel artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9ddf3443317e4f7f6bbc28748bb239810d178fd4, describing c086ee1e, to be covered by this head, name it in the verdict artifact set or retire it yourself. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}